\name{readers_read_spectra}
\alias{readers_read_spectra}
\title{Read the scans of a spectra file: headers always, peaks on request}
\usage{
readers_read_spectra(path, limit = NULL, offset = 0, ms_order = NULL, peaks = FALSE,
  out = NULL, timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a spectra file. A Bruker \code{.d} directory is also accepted.}

\item{limit}{Maximum scans to return. \code{NULL}, the default, returns all of them.}

\item{offset}{Scans to skip, applied \strong{after} \code{ms_order}.}

\item{ms_order}{Keep only scans at this MS level - \code{1} for survey scans, \code{2} for fragment scans.
  \code{NULL}, the default, keeps every scan. Applied before \code{offset} and \code{limit}, so
  \code{ms_order = 2, limit = 10} means the first ten MS2 scans rather than the MS2 scans among the
  first ten.}

\item{peaks}{Include the \code{mz} and \code{intensity} arrays. \code{FALSE} by default, and worth leaving so
  unless you need them: a scan header is tens of bytes and its peak list is thousands, and a
  mid-size mzML holds tens of thousands of scans. \code{peak_count} still reports how many peaks each
  scan has.}

\item{out}{Write a tab-separated table here and return only a summary. With \code{peaks = TRUE} each
  cell holds a \code{;}-joined list.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely. A large \code{.raw} legitimately
  takes a while.
}

}
\description{
Seven file types offer the \code{spectra} view: \code{.mzML}, \code{.mgf}, \code{_ms1.msalign}, \code{_ms2.msalign},
Thermo \code{.raw}, Bruker \code{.d} and timsTOF \code{.d}.
}
\details{
Retention times here \strong{are} in minutes for every format - mzLib's spectra readers convert at
the boundary, unlike its result-file readers, which pass the tool's own unit through untouched.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_scan_records}. \code{scan_count} is the file's total \strong{before} any \code{ms_order}
  filter, reported alongside \code{record_count} so a filter that matched nothing can never look like
  an empty file.

}
\section{Two of the seven need Windows}{


Bruker \code{.d} and timsTOF \code{.d} are read through vendor native libraries (\code{baf2sql}, \code{timsdata})
and are \strong{Windows-x64 only}. Thermo \code{.raw} uses managed vendor assemblies and works everywhere.
msalign files hold \strong{deconvolved neutral masses}, not raw m/z - do not re-deconvolve them.

}
